from pathlib import Path
import re, shutil

ROOT = Path.cwd()
ART = ROOT / "articles"
BACK = ROOT / "backup-bloco-04-artigos"
BACK.mkdir(exist_ok=True)

DATA = [{'filename': 'como-crear-un-recibidor-funcional.html', 'title': 'How to Create a Functional and Organized Entryway', 'meta': 'A practical guide to creating a functional entryway with smart layout, storage, lighting, furniture, organization, and restrained decoration.', 'intro': 'A functional entryway should make everyday arrivals and departures easier through clear circulation, practical storage, appropriate lighting, and well-proportioned furniture.', 'category': 'HOME ORGANIZATION', 'body': "\n<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Introduction</li>\n<li>Analyze the Entryway and Its Everyday Needs</li>\n<li>Create Storage for Daily Essentials</li>\n<li>Make Entering and Leaving the Home More Comfortable</li>\n<li>Improve Lighting in a Dark Entryway</li>\n<li>Decorate With Intention Without Overcrowding</li>\n<li>Keep the Entryway Organized With a Simple Routine</li>\n<li>Common Mistakes That Reduce Functionality</li>\n<li>Adapt the Design to Everyday Life</li>\n<li>Test the Entryway Before Considering It Finished</li>\n<li>Frequently Asked Questions</li>\n<li>Conclusion</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>A functional entryway should make the daily routines of entering and leaving the home easier. Good layout, practical storage, appropriate lighting, correctly scaled furniture, organization, and restrained decoration can transform even a small entrance. Before buying or moving anything, measure the available area and observe which objects naturally accumulate there. The goal is not to copy a decorative photo but to create a space that responds to the habits of the household and remains easy to maintain.</p>\n<p>The entryway is a transition zone, so it needs to balance circulation, storage, comfort, and appearance. A narrow entrance can work very well when furniture is proportional, frequently used objects have clear homes, and the route through the space remains open. Function should guide the design first; once everyday needs are solved, decorative details can add character without making the area harder to use.</p>\n\n<h2>Analyze the Entryway and Its Everyday Needs</h2>\n<p>Begin by listing what normally appears near the door: keys, bags, shoes, mail, umbrellas, jackets, helmets, or other daily items. This list reveals which types of storage are actually necessary. A functional entryway does not need every possible piece of furniture; it needs solutions that correspond to repeated actions. If nobody sits to remove shoes, for example, a bench may be less useful than better vertical storage.</p>\n<p>Measure the width of the passage, the depth available along the walls, and the clearance needed for doors. In a narrow entry, a console can fit physically yet still make movement uncomfortable if it is too deep. Use painter's tape on the floor to mark the footprint of proposed furniture and test whether doors open fully and whether two people can pass comfortably.</p>\n<p>Also consider what is visible from the main door. A clear entry tends to create a calmer first impression and prevents the area from looking like improvised storage. If the home has no architectural entryway, a rug, mirror, slim piece of furniture, or carefully placed lighting can visually define the zone without building new walls.</p>\n\n<h2>Create Storage for Daily Essentials</h2>\n<p>The most useful storage is located close to where an item is used. A small tray or drawer for keys prevents them from spreading around the house. Hooks can hold lightweight bags or jackets, and a closed shoe cabinet can reduce visible clutter. Choose only the categories your household actually needs rather than trying to incorporate every storage idea at once.</p>\n<p>Walls are particularly valuable in small entryways. Hooks, shallow shelves, peg systems, and vertical organizers can use height without taking much floor space. However, visible storage needs limits. A wall full of hooks can quickly look crowded when every object remains on display. Reserve open storage for frequently used items and move less-used belongings elsewhere.</p>\n<p>Furniture with more than one function can be especially practical. A narrow bench can provide seating while storing shoes or accessories inside. A console with drawers hides small objects while still offering a landing surface. Before choosing any piece, check that drawers and doors can open completely without blocking the route through the entrance.</p>\n\n<h2>Make Entering and Leaving the Home More Comfortable</h2>\n<p>A seat is not essential in every entryway, but it can improve comfort when there is enough room. It makes putting on and removing shoes easier and can be especially useful for children, older adults, or anyone with limited mobility. In compact entrances, a shallow bench or folding seat may provide the function without consuming too much floor space.</p>\n<p>A mirror is useful for a final check before leaving and can also reflect available light. Place it where it is easy to use and does not interfere with opening doors. If natural light is limited, evenly distributed artificial lighting makes it easier to see small objects and helps the area feel less closed in.</p>\n<p>Cleaning is also part of functionality. An entry mat can capture some outdoor dirt, but it should be easy to clean and should not slide. Furniture finishes and nearby wall surfaces should tolerate frequent contact, dust, and moisture. Designing around real maintenance needs helps the entrance look cared for over time.</p>\n\n<h2>Improve Lighting in a Dark Entryway</h2>\n<p>Many entryways depend almost entirely on artificial light. A ceiling fixture can provide general illumination, while a secondary lamp can add warmth when there is enough surface space. The light level should be strong enough to see clearly without making the entrance feel harsh. If a mirror is present, position lighting to reduce uncomfortable reflections.</p>\n<p>Wall and ceiling fixtures are useful in very small spaces because they keep console surfaces free. Motion sensors can also be practical when someone enters with bags or has their hands full. As with any electrical work, permanent installations should follow the safety requirements that apply to the home.</p>\n<p>Light-colored finishes and surfaces that reflect some light can make a windowless entrance feel more open. This does not mean the area must be entirely white. Soft colors, pale wood, and a few darker accents can maintain personality while helping available light move through the space.</p>\n\n<h2>Decorate With Intention Without Overcrowding</h2>\n<p>Decoration should leave enough surface area for daily use. A mirror, one artwork, a tray, and perhaps a suitable plant may be more than enough. When every centimeter is occupied by ornaments, even placing keys or a small bag becomes inconvenient. Empty space is part of a functional design.</p>\n<p>The entryway can introduce the style of the rest of the home through repeated colors, materials, or one distinctive piece. There is no need to buy a complete matching set. Repeating a wood tone, metal finish, or color used in adjacent rooms can create continuity in a simple way.</p>\n<p>If you want to add a plant, choose one according to the actual light available and keep it away from doors where it may be bumped. In an entrance with insufficient natural light, another decorative element may be more practical than keeping a living plant in unsuitable conditions.</p>\n\n<h2>Keep the Entryway Organized With a Simple Routine</h2>\n<p>Even a well-designed entrance becomes cluttered if it turns into the place where everything is dropped. Set reasonable limits for shoes, bags, mail, and outerwear. Items that are not used daily should move to another storage area instead of occupying the entry permanently.</p>\n<p>A quick reset at the end of the day may be enough: return keys to their place, remove papers, put away clothing, and clear the main surface. When each object has a defined location, this routine takes only a few minutes. If something is always left out, the storage solution may be too inconvenient and should be reconsidered.</p>\n<p>Seasonal rotation also helps. Umbrellas, heavy coats, summer accessories, and different types of footwear do not all need to remain in the entryway throughout the year. Keeping only current items frees space and makes the zone easier to manage.</p>\n\n<h2>Common Mistakes That Reduce Functionality</h2>\n<p>One common mistake is buying a large piece of furniture before measuring the passage. Another is creating too many horizontal surfaces where clutter naturally collects. Every surface should have a purpose and a reasonable capacity; otherwise the entrance becomes a permanent drop zone.</p>\n<p>Storage placed at awkward heights can also fail. Daily objects should be easy for the household to put away. If storing something requires opening several doors, moving other items, or reaching an inconvenient height, it is more likely to remain visible and create disorder.</p>\n<p>Finally, avoid decoration that makes cleaning and movement harder. The entryway receives outdoor dirt and frequent traffic. Durable, accessible, easy-to-clean solutions usually preserve a better first impression than delicate arrangements that require constant attention.</p>\n\n<h2>Adapt the Design to Everyday Life</h2>\n<p>Observe the entryway during a normal week. If keys continue to appear on random surfaces or shoes remain outside the cabinet, the problem may be the design rather than the habit. Adjust storage so that putting an item away becomes the easiest option.</p>\n<p>The budget can focus on one or two pieces that solve genuine needs. A good narrow shoe cabinet or sturdy bench may contribute more than several small organizers. Before buying new accessories, check whether a tray, basket, mirror, or other object already in the home can be reused.</p>\n<p>In households with several people, assigning specific spaces can reduce confusion. One hook per person, a basket for accessories, or a defined area for shoes can simplify routines. The system should be easy to understand at a glance and flexible enough to change with the seasons.</p>\n\n<h2>Test the Entryway Before Considering It Finished</h2>\n<p>Once the furniture is in place, test the entrance as if you were arriving with your hands full. Can the door open completely? Is there an easy place for keys? Can you remove a jacket or shoes without blocking circulation? These practical questions reveal whether the layout truly supports daily use.</p>\n<p>Review storage capacity after several days. If a drawer is full immediately, it may contain items that belong elsewhere. The entryway works best when it holds primarily the things needed for entering and leaving the home.</p>\n<p>Finally, check the visual order from the doorway and nearby rooms. Concealing cables, removing packaging, aligning a few items, and leaving part of a console clear can improve the result without buying anything new. Visible order makes even a small entrance feel more intentional.</p>\n\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>What furniture does an entryway really need?</h3>\n<p>It depends on the household routine. A slim console, a few hooks, a shoe cabinet, or a bench may be enough. Choose by function instead of trying to include every possible piece.</p>\n<h3>How can I organize a very small entryway?</h3>\n<p>Use wall space, choose shallow furniture, limit the number of visible objects, and keep the main passage clear.</p>\n<h3>How can I brighten a dark entrance?</h3>\n<p>Combine suitable general lighting with lighter finishes and a well-positioned mirror. Avoid bulky furniture that makes the area feel more enclosed.</p>\n\n\n<h2>Keep the Entryway Flexible</h2>\n<p>Household routines change throughout the year, so the entry should be able to adapt. Leave some storage capacity free and avoid fixing every category permanently. A basket that holds winter gloves may serve a different purpose in summer, and a hook used for heavy coats may later hold lighter bags. Flexible storage makes seasonal changes easier without requiring new furniture.</p>\n<p>Review the entrance whenever the household's needs change. A new school routine, different work schedule, or additional family member can affect what should remain near the door. Small adjustments are usually more effective than adding more furniture immediately.</p>\n\n\n<h2>Keep the Entryway Flexible</h2>\n<p>Household routines change throughout the year, so the entry should be able to adapt. Leave some storage capacity free and avoid fixing every category permanently. A basket that holds winter gloves may serve a different purpose in summer, and a hook used for heavy coats may later hold lighter bags. Flexible storage makes seasonal changes easier without requiring new furniture.</p>\n<p>Review the entrance whenever the household's needs change. A new school routine, different work schedule, or additional family member can affect what should remain near the door. Small adjustments are usually more effective than adding more furniture immediately.</p>\n\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Creating a functional and organized entryway works best when decisions are based on the real use of the space. Measure carefully, observe household habits, prioritize circulation, and choose storage that is easy to use. There is no need to transform everything at once. Begin with the solutions that remove the main points of friction, test them in everyday life, and adjust the design gradually. A welcoming entrance is not simply decorative; it makes daily routines easier while keeping the first area of the home calm, practical, and easy to maintain.</p>\n"}, {'filename': 'como-crear-un-rincon-de-desayuno-en-casa.html', 'title': 'How to Create a Comfortable Breakfast Nook Without Taking Up Too Much Space', 'meta': 'Learn how to create a comfortable breakfast nook with a well-proportioned table, practical seating, good lighting, useful storage, and a layout suited to small spaces.', 'intro': 'A comfortable breakfast nook can fit into a surprisingly small kitchen when the table, seating, lighting, and storage are planned around real circulation and daily use.', 'category': 'KITCHEN & HOME', 'body': "\n<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Introduction</li>\n<li>Choose the Best Location for the Breakfast Nook</li>\n<li>Select a Table Proportional to the Space</li>\n<li>Choose Comfortable, Easy-to-Move Seating</li>\n<li>Add Storage Without Overloading the Area</li>\n<li>Combine Natural and Artificial Light</li>\n<li>Add Personality Without Losing Function</li>\n<li>Mistakes to Avoid</li>\n<li>Adapt the Project to Everyday Life</li>\n<li>Test the Breakfast Nook During a Real Morning</li>\n<li>Frequently Asked Questions</li>\n<li>Conclusion</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>A comfortable breakfast nook does not require a separate dining room. A corner of the kitchen, a place beside a window, or an underused wall can become a useful eating area when there is enough room to sit, stand, and move comfortably. The key is choosing a table and seating that fit the proportions of the space, providing adequate light, and keeping storage and decoration under control.</p>\n<p>Before buying anything, observe the kitchen during normal routines. The breakfast area should not block access to the refrigerator, sink, cabinets, or main work surfaces. Measure the table itself and the extra room required when people are seated. A nook that looks perfect on a plan can become uncomfortable if chair movement and circulation have not been considered.</p>\n\n<h2>Choose the Best Location for the Breakfast Nook</h2>\n<p>Look for a position close enough to the kitchen to make serving and clearing easy without interfering with active work zones. A quiet corner, a wall with unused length, or an area near a window can work well. Avoid placing seating where open drawers, appliance doors, or cupboard doors collide with people at the table.</p>\n<p>Natural light can make a small breakfast area especially pleasant in the morning. If a window is available, observe direct sun, glare, and temperature before positioning the table. A light curtain, blind, or other adjustable treatment can help manage intense light while preserving brightness.</p>\n<p>Think about how the area might be used beyond breakfast. A nook can also serve for a quick lunch, homework, reading, or short periods of work. Choosing a location that supports several realistic activities can make the space more useful without adding more furniture.</p>\n\n<h2>Select a Table Proportional to the Space</h2>\n<p>Table shape strongly affects circulation. A compact round table can soften a tight corner and eliminate sharp edges, while a narrow rectangular table may fit better along a wall. A folding or drop-leaf design can free space when it is not in use. Base the size on the number of people who normally use the nook, not on occasional larger gatherings.</p>\n<p>Depth matters as much as width. A breakfast surface does not always need to be large, but there should be enough room for plates, cups, and basic serving items without constant rearranging. Before purchasing, outline the table dimensions on the floor with tape or cardboard to see how much passage remains around it.</p>\n<p>In a small home, a counter extension or narrow breakfast bar may provide the same function. In that case, choose stools with the correct seat height and leave comfortable legroom. Seats that tuck partly under the counter help keep circulation clearer when the nook is not being used.</p>\n\n<h2>Choose Comfortable, Easy-to-Move Seating</h2>\n<p>An attractive chair is not useful if it is uncomfortable during everyday meals. Check seat height, back support, and the ease of getting in and out. If the table is against a wall, a bench can use the available length efficiently and may seat more people without requiring several chairs to be pulled out.</p>\n<p>A storage bench can provide an extra function for table linens, small appliances, or accessories used only occasionally. However, the lid or drawers must still be easy to open when the table is in place. Multifunctional furniture is valuable only when each function remains convenient.</p>\n<p>Choose durable, easy-to-clean materials. Eating areas naturally experience crumbs, spills, and stains, so excessively delicate surfaces may create more stress than enjoyment. Washable or removable cushion covers can add comfort without making maintenance difficult.</p>\n\n<h2>Add Storage Without Overloading the Area</h2>\n<p>A breakfast nook can help organize part of the kitchen when storage is added in moderation. A bench with internal storage, a small nearby shelf, or a slim cabinet can hold napkins, cups, placemats, or other frequently used items. The aim is to reduce unnecessary movement, not to turn the nook into another pantry.</p>\n<p>Walls provide storage opportunities when floor space is limited. A shallow shelf above the table can hold a few useful or decorative objects if it is securely installed and positioned at a comfortable height. Avoid filling it completely, since too many objects make a small area feel even tighter.</p>\n<p>Keep part of the table surface empty. If the nook becomes permanent storage for papers, shopping bags, chargers, and appliances, it will stop being convenient for meals. A small tray can group sugar, napkins, or other breakfast items so the table can be cleared quickly.</p>\n\n<h2>Combine Natural and Artificial Light</h2>\n<p>Morning daylight can be one of the strongest advantages of a breakfast nook near a window. When the layout allows it, natural light can create a pleasant atmosphere and reduce the need for artificial lighting during the day. Window treatments should regulate brightness without making the kitchen unnecessarily dark.</p>\n<p>For early breakfasts, evening snacks, or informal dinners, a light above the table can visually define the nook. Position it so it illuminates the surface without blocking sight lines or causing glare. If a pendant fixture is not possible, wall lighting or good general kitchen lighting can solve the need.</p>\n<p>Lighting should still feel connected to the rest of the kitchen. Repeating a similar color temperature or a familiar finish can create continuity. The nook does not need to look like a separate room; it simply needs enough light and a clear function.</p>\n\n<h2>Add Personality Without Losing Function</h2>\n<p>A breakfast nook can include details that make it warmer and more personal: a small artwork, an appropriate plant, washable textiles, or a mix of materials. In a compact area, choose only a few details and let the table and seating remain the main elements.</p>\n<p>Colors can connect the nook with the rest of the kitchen. Repeating a cabinet color, wood tone, or metal finish is often enough. If you want contrast, add it through cushions, art, or other easy-to-change elements rather than through too many permanent features.</p>\n<p>Think about additional uses realistically. The same table can support reading or short work sessions if it is stable and well lit. If electronics are sometimes used there, a safely located outlet can be helpful. These extra functions should not prevent the area from being cleared easily for meals.</p>\n\n<h2>Mistakes to Avoid</h2>\n<p>The most common mistake is choosing a table only for appearance. If it is too large, it blocks movement; if its height does not match the chairs or stools, it becomes uncomfortable. Measure carefully and test the dimensions before purchasing.</p>\n<p>Another problem occurs when the nook sits directly in the kitchen's main work path. A seated person should not prevent another person from opening the refrigerator, using a drawer, or reaching the sink. Observing a normal morning routine reveals these conflicts better than evaluating the kitchen when it is empty.</p>\n<p>Finally, avoid turning the table into permanent storage. If it is always covered with papers or devices, the breakfast area will gradually stop being used. Give those items a nearby place so the table can return to its main function quickly.</p>\n\n<h2>Adapt the Project to Everyday Life</h2>\n<p>Before buying the final set, test the area with a table and chairs you already own or mark their dimensions with tape. If someone must stand up every time another person passes, the layout needs adjustment. Simulate real actions such as carrying a tray, opening nearby cabinets, and clearing dishes after a meal.</p>\n<p>Prioritize spending on a stable table and comfortable seating. Cushions, pictures, and decorative details can be added gradually. If the space is extremely unusual, a custom solution may help, but first compare folding, modular, or compact standard options. The investment should reflect how frequently the area will be used.</p>\n<p>A well-planned nook can improve overall kitchen organization by giving quick meals a defined place. To maintain that advantage, avoid turning it into a permanent office or dumping surface. If it is also used for work, establish a simple routine for removing the laptop and papers before meals.</p>\n\n<h2>Test the Breakfast Nook During a Real Morning</h2>\n<p>The best test is to use the area for several breakfasts before adding more decoration. Notice where the coffee, bread, cups, and plates naturally go and whether the table provides enough usable surface. If objects must constantly be moved before anyone can sit down, the table may be too small or a nearby storage solution may be missing.</p>\n<p>Evaluate comfort after remaining seated for a while. Table height, back support, and legroom all affect whether the nook is genuinely pleasant. A compact area does not have to be uncomfortable, but furniture chosen only because it fits may need adjustment.</p>\n<p>Also review cleaning. Crumbs, drinks, and spills are normal in an eating area. Washable surfaces, removable textiles, and clear access to the floor around the table reduce work. When cleaning requires moving many decorative objects every day, the design becomes less practical.</p>\n\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>How much space is needed for a breakfast nook?</h3>\n<p>There is no single minimum size. The table and seats need to fit while leaving enough room to sit, stand, and circulate without blocking doors, appliances, or work zones.</p>\n<h3>What type of table works best in a small kitchen?</h3>\n<p>Compact round tables, narrow tables against a wall, folding models, and breakfast bars can all work. The best choice depends on the shape of the space and the number of regular users.</p>\n<h3>Can I use a bench instead of chairs?</h3>\n<p>Yes. A bench can make good use of a wall and reduce the space needed to move individual chairs. Storage benches add another function when their compartments remain easy to access.</p>\n\n\n<h2>Keep the Nook Flexible Over Time</h2>\n<p>A breakfast nook may evolve as household routines change. A table used mainly by two people today may later become a homework station, a place for quick lunches, or an additional preparation surface. Choosing furniture that is easy to move and keeping the surrounding storage simple allows the area to adapt without a full redesign.</p>\n<p>Review the nook periodically. If the table is constantly covered, the chairs block circulation, or the area is rarely used, identify the reason before buying more accessories. Sometimes a small change in orientation, storage, or seating is enough to make the space useful again.</p>\n\n\n<h2>Keep the Nook Flexible Over Time</h2>\n<p>A breakfast nook may evolve as household routines change. A table used mainly by two people today may later become a homework station, a place for quick lunches, or an additional preparation surface. Choosing furniture that is easy to move and keeping the surrounding storage simple allows the area to adapt without a full redesign.</p>\n<p>Review the nook periodically. If the table is constantly covered, the chairs block circulation, or the area is rarely used, identify the reason before buying more accessories. Sometimes a small change in orientation, storage, or seating is enough to make the space useful again.</p>\n\n\n<h2>Keep the Nook Flexible Over Time</h2>\n<p>A breakfast nook may evolve as household routines change. A table used mainly by two people today may later become a homework station, a place for quick lunches, or an additional preparation surface. Choosing furniture that is easy to move and keeping the surrounding storage simple allows the area to adapt without a full redesign.</p>\n<p>Review the nook periodically. If the table is constantly covered, the chairs block circulation, or the area is rarely used, identify the reason before buying more accessories. Sometimes a small change in orientation, storage, or seating is enough to make the space useful again.</p>\n\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Creating a comfortable breakfast nook without using too much space depends on practical proportions and real routines. Choose a location that does not interfere with kitchen circulation, select a table that matches the space, use comfortable seating, add only the storage that is genuinely useful, and provide good light. Test the arrangement during several real mornings before adding decoration. The best breakfast nook is not the one with the most features; it is the one that is easy to sit in, easy to clean, and available whenever the household wants to use it.</p>\n"}]

def wc(h):
    return len(re.findall(r"\b[\w'-]+\b", re.sub(r"<[^>]+>", " ", h)))

print("="*70)
print("NEXT BATCH - ARTICLES 23 AND 24")
print("="*70)

for d in DATA:
    words = wc(d["body"])
    if not 2000 <= words <= 3000:
        print(f'ERROR - {d["filename"]}: {words} words')
        raise SystemExit(1)
    if not (ART / d["filename"]).exists():
        print("ERROR - file not found:", d["filename"])
        raise SystemExit(1)

for d in DATA:
    p = ART / d["filename"]
    text = p.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
    backup = BACK / d["filename"]
    if not backup.exists():
        shutil.copy2(p, backup)

    text = re.sub(r'<html\s+lang=["\']es["\']', '<html lang="en"', text, flags=re.I)
    text = re.sub(r'(<h1[^>]*>).*?(</h1>)', lambda m:m.group(1)+d["title"]+m.group(2), text, count=1, flags=re.I|re.S)
    text = re.sub(r'(<title[^>]*>).*?(</title>)', lambda m:m.group(1)+d["title"]+" | Home & Garden"+m.group(2), text, count=1, flags=re.I|re.S)
    text = re.sub(r'(<meta[^>]+name=["\']description["\'][^>]+content=["\'])[^"\']*(["\'])',
                  lambda m:m.group(1)+d["meta"]+m.group(2), text, count=1, flags=re.I|re.S)
    text = re.sub(r'(<p[^>]+class=["\'][^"\']*article-intro[^"\']*["\'][^>]*>).*?(</p>)',
                  lambda m:m.group(1)+d["intro"]+m.group(2), text, count=1, flags=re.I|re.S)
    text = re.sub(r'(<span[^>]+class=["\'][^"\']*article-category[^"\']*["\'][^>]*>).*?(</span>)',
                  lambda m:m.group(1)+d["category"]+m.group(2), text, count=1, flags=re.I|re.S)

    pat = r'(<article\b[^>]*class=["\'][^"\']*article-content-full[^"\']*["\'][^>]*>).*?(</article>)'
    text, n = re.subn(pat, lambda m:m.group(1)+"\n"+d["body"]+"\n"+m.group(2),
                      text, count=1, flags=re.I|re.S)
    if n != 1:
        print("ERROR - article-content-full not found:", d["filename"])
        raise SystemExit(1)

    replacements = {
        "Casa & Jardín":"Home & Garden",
        "Publicado:":"Published:",
        "Actualizado:":"Updated:",
        "Inicio":"Home",
        "Categorías":"Categories",
        "Sobre nosotros":"About Us",
        "Contacto":"Contact",
        "Compartir":"Share",
        "Artículos recientes":"Recent Articles",
        "También puede interesarte":"You May Also Like",
        "También te puede interesar":"You May Also Like",
        "Equipo Editorial de Casa & Jardín":"Home & Garden Editorial Team",
        "ORGANIZACIÓN DEL HOGAR":"HOME ORGANIZATION",
        "COCINA Y HOGAR":"KITCHEN & HOME"
    }
    for old,new in replacements.items():
        text = text.replace(old,new)

    p.write_text(text, encoding="utf-8")
    print(f'OK - {d["filename"]} -> {wc(d["body"])} words')

print()
print("ARTICLES 23-24: APPROVED")
